{"exhaustive":{"nbHits":false,"typo":false},"exhaustiveNbHits":false,"exhaustiveTypo":false,"hits":[{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"srid68"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["claude","code","pricing"],"value":"1-year part-time experiment: can you build a large project with AI <em>writing</em> the <em>code</em> and AI plus deterministic tools verifying/guiding/enforcing it for structural and behavior consistency, without a human reading the <em>code</em>?<p>I'm building a polyglot web framework in 15 languages, with C# and Lean4 as the reference implementations. Structural and behavioral audits using deterministic tools check that every port stays consistent with the reference. It's about 1.8M lines now. I have not reviewed the <em>code</em>/neither have i verified the behavior tests except at the time of giving requirement and never seen some of the language <em>code</em> at all.<p>The question is how far can we produce &quot;working <em>code</em> \u2192 correct <em>code</em> [Possible] \u2192 good <em>code</em> [Unknown]&quot; with deterministic audits correcting structure, ai mistakes and behavior validation.<p>So far the quality looks acceptable with lots of correction of AI output done without looking, but it isn't fully verified.<p>Line counts per language, with changes since August: <a href=\"https://claude.ai/artifact/5f1m1pTM7tZ4boQzwsoAG7\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://<em>claude</em>.ai/artifact/5f1m1pTM7tZ4boQzwsoAG7</a>"},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Ask HN: What are you working on? (November 2026)"}},"_tags":["comment","author_srid68","story_50328340"],"author":"srid68","children":[50330513],"comment_text":"1-year part-time experiment: can you build a large project with AI writing the code and AI plus deterministic tools verifying&#x2F;guiding&#x2F;enforcing it for structural and behavior consistency, without a human reading the code?<p>I&#x27;m building a polyglot web framework in 15 languages, with C# and Lean4 as the reference implementations. Structural and behavioral audits using deterministic tools check that every port stays consistent with the reference. It&#x27;s about 1.8M lines now. I have not reviewed the code&#x2F;neither have i verified the behavior tests except at the time of giving requirement and never seen some of the language code at all.<p>The question is how far can we produce &quot;working code \u2192 correct code [Possible] \u2192 good code [Unknown]&quot; with deterministic audits correcting structure, ai mistakes and behavior validation.<p>So far the quality looks acceptable with lots of correction of AI output done without looking, but it isn&#x27;t fully verified.<p>Line counts per language, with changes since August: <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;claude.ai&#x2F;artifact&#x2F;5f1m1pTM7tZ4boQzwsoAG7\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;claude.ai&#x2F;artifact&#x2F;5f1m1pTM7tZ4boQzwsoAG7</a>","created_at":"2026-11-01T21:37:31Z","created_at_i":1793569051,"objectID":"50330163","parent_id":50328340,"story_id":50328340,"story_title":"Ask HN: What are you working on? (November 2026)","updated_at":"2026-11-01T23:03:10Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"minraws"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["claude","code","pricing"],"value":"As much as I would like to say I agree, I don't really like the LLM ui replacements. I absolutely hate them, they are just horrible, <em>claude</em> <em>code</em> itself is a horrible way of producing <em>code</em>/output you aren't seeing the logical pieces of your project you are largely stuck in a text box with a diff viewer and unable to parse what's happening.<p>Something like codex desktop is slightly better but still a horrible interface for developing software in any form of serious capacity.<p>Sure it works because the friction of manually working is just so damn high. And there aren't good alternatives, the jump from pi to <em>claude</em> to codex being almost negligible for most folks is a very high signal this entire pattern just doesn't really work.<p>I don't yet know where we will land or will we suffer with the current bad type text in a box ux for the next few years but I am certain this is not it.<p>I am still trying to work out what is it though, so well we will see when we get there.<p>Not using tools is a good example of the issue, you give up a lot of control to do that, sometimes it's good enough often it's not, and often it's worse because you don't even realize how much control you have given up.<p>I don't think we will do natural language interactions with our computers, I honestly feel like we will do something more intent based. If LLMs can know from our words what we want to do, then they can certain infer from what we are doing stuff we are about to do.<p>Think auto complete but for user actions, without taking away user control and understanding intent almost perfectly. Maybe it would require neuralink or something to get good enough, but until that happens I don't think we are moving away entirely from our UIs/UXs entirely.<p>Some people will, the low skill, low requirements, but no-<em>code</em> has existed for a very long time for the same reason, visual scripting is a similar example.<p>A video made by LLMs by using Premiere Pro is always going to be worse than what a professional makes entirely on their own in Premiere Pro not in terms of quality but in terms of control hence intent.<p>Similar issues with software, you can not perfectly describe what you want to another person perfectly that just doesn't happen, otherwise we wouldn't have had companies with more managers than engineers it seems like.<p>There is a lot of loss of intent, imagine if every action was something where you lost some of your intent, then after 100 requests does any of your intent even remains in it.?<p>This might be too hypothetical but I don't see LLMs replacing UI in the short term, but I don't know what happens in longer term.<p>But I do think LLMs will change how the software the interfaces and tooling works almost entirely.<p>Today it's much easier to llm rather than sql. It's not even a question of whether it's 10% better or something the cognitive load of <em>writing</em> SQL is high enough in data analysis that LLMs are the only solution.<p>LLMs enable some pretty interesting ways to query data itself, you can ask the data to query itself, imagine being able to prompt inject the data.<p>But on a more useful case imagine being able to let data build itself, when ever an incident happens, LLMs can enable flags to collect more data automatically based on incidents add reporting apis and telemetry and so much more.<p>Incident analysis should have changed but it hasn't yet, a human for some random reason still needs to ask an LLM to do the work, then understand it somewhat and then reply to others."},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Pi Is the Last Harness"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://destiner.io/blog/post/pi-is-the-last-harness/"}},"_tags":["comment","author_minraws","story_50326025"],"author":"minraws","children":[50328591],"comment_text":"As much as I would like to say I agree, I don&#x27;t really like the LLM ui replacements. I absolutely hate them, they are just horrible, claude code itself is a horrible way of producing code&#x2F;output you aren&#x27;t seeing the logical pieces of your project you are largely stuck in a text box with a diff viewer and unable to parse what&#x27;s happening.<p>Something like codex desktop is slightly better but still a horrible interface for developing software in any form of serious capacity.<p>Sure it works because the friction of manually working is just so damn high. And there aren&#x27;t good alternatives, the jump from pi to claude to codex being almost negligible for most folks is a very high signal this entire pattern just doesn&#x27;t really work.<p>I don&#x27;t yet know where we will land or will we suffer with the current bad type text in a box ux for the next few years but I am certain this is not it.<p>I am still trying to work out what is it though, so well we will see when we get there.<p>Not using tools is a good example of the issue, you give up a lot of control to do that, sometimes it&#x27;s good enough often it&#x27;s not, and often it&#x27;s worse because you don&#x27;t even realize how much control you have given up.<p>I don&#x27;t think we will do natural language interactions with our computers, I honestly feel like we will do something more intent based. If LLMs can know from our words what we want to do, then they can certain infer from what we are doing stuff we are about to do.<p>Think auto complete but for user actions, without taking away user control and understanding intent almost perfectly. Maybe it would require neuralink or something to get good enough, but until that happens I don&#x27;t think we are moving away entirely from our UIs&#x2F;UXs entirely.<p>Some people will, the low skill, low requirements, but no-code has existed for a very long time for the same reason, visual scripting is a similar example.<p>A video made by LLMs by using Premiere Pro is always going to be worse than what a professional makes entirely on their own in Premiere Pro not in terms of quality but in terms of control hence intent.<p>Similar issues with software, you can not perfectly describe what you want to another person perfectly that just doesn&#x27;t happen, otherwise we wouldn&#x27;t have had companies with more managers than engineers it seems like.<p>There is a lot of loss of intent, imagine if every action was something where you lost some of your intent, then after 100 requests does any of your intent even remains in it.?<p>This might be too hypothetical but I don&#x27;t see LLMs replacing UI in the short term, but I don&#x27;t know what happens in longer term.<p>But I do think LLMs will change how the software the interfaces and tooling works almost entirely.<p>Today it&#x27;s much easier to llm rather than sql. It&#x27;s not even a question of whether it&#x27;s 10% better or something the cognitive load of writing SQL is high enough in data analysis that LLMs are the only solution.<p>LLMs enable some pretty interesting ways to query data itself, you can ask the data to query itself, imagine being able to prompt inject the data.<p>But on a more useful case imagine being able to let data build itself, when ever an incident happens, LLMs can enable flags to collect more data automatically based on incidents add reporting apis and telemetry and so much more.<p>Incident analysis should have changed but it hasn&#x27;t yet, a human for some random reason still needs to ask an LLM to do the work, then understand it somewhat and then reply to others.","created_at":"2026-11-01T17:08:31Z","created_at_i":1793552911,"objectID":"50327486","parent_id":50327216,"story_id":50326025,"story_title":"Pi Is the Last Harness","story_url":"https://destiner.io/blog/post/pi-is-the-last-harness/","updated_at":"2026-11-01T19:15:10Z"},{"_highlightResult":{"author":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Wowfunhappy"},"comment_text":{"fullyHighlighted":false,"matchLevel":"full","matchedWords":["claude","code","pricing"],"value":"&gt; They have made a pragmatic decision already to cut loose a whole swathe of users so they can pursue areas higher up the value chain. that was essentially the original decision they made when they went after software engineering instead of general users.<p>...I don't think I agree that this happened. <em>Claude</em> Cowork exists, as does <em>Claude</em> Design.<p>My read of Anthropic's strategy is to focus on coding as a means of achieving capability in other domains, via <em>writing</em> <em>code</em>. I would say that's paying off: see <em>Claude</em> Opus 5.5 creating a music video, via building animations in Javascript. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CS8ro03rJOM\" rel=\"nofollow\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CS8ro03rJOM</a><p>By <em>writing</em> bits of <em>code</em>, <em>Claude</em> can organize your calendar, create vector graphics, organize your tasks, and so on.<p>If what you mean is that newer <em>Claude</em> models are worse at creative <em>writing</em>\u2014okay, but to what extent were LLMs ever seriously useful for this, beyond generating slop no one actually wants to read? Opus 3 is still available in <em>claude</em>.ai if you want to use it.<p>---<p>Now, separately, I do think that Anthropic is internally <i>obsessed</i> with &quot;existential risk&quot;, to the point where they will cut off users. I do think this is genuine based on everything we've heard from Anthropic employees.<p>And of course, this safety culture likely persists in part because it's <i>also</i> useful to the company's overall strategy, in the same way Apple's focus on user privacy, while likely genuine, conveniently lets them maintain control of the App Store."},"story_title":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"Anthropic blocks users for anything touching training larger models"},"story_url":{"matchLevel":"none","matchedWords":[],"value":"https://twitter.com/jiahaoshao1/status/2116475922190922088"}},"_tags":["comment","author_Wowfunhappy","story_50319358"],"author":"Wowfunhappy","comment_text":"&gt; They have made a pragmatic decision already to cut loose a whole swathe of users so they can pursue areas higher up the value chain. that was essentially the original decision they made when they went after software engineering instead of general users.<p>...I don&#x27;t think I agree that this happened. Claude Cowork exists, as does Claude Design.<p>My read of Anthropic&#x27;s strategy is to focus on coding as a means of achieving capability in other domains, via writing code. I would say that&#x27;s paying off: see Claude Opus 5.5 creating a music video, via building animations in Javascript. <a href=\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.youtube.com&#x2F;watch?v=CS8ro03rJOM\" rel=\"nofollow\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.youtube.com&#x2F;watch?v=CS8ro03rJOM</a><p>By writing bits of code, Claude can organize your calendar, create vector graphics, organize your tasks, and so on.<p>If what you mean is that newer Claude models are worse at creative writing\u2014okay, but to what extent were LLMs ever seriously useful for this, beyond generating slop no one actually wants to read? Opus 3 is still available in claude.ai if you want to use it.<p>---<p>Now, separately, I do think that Anthropic is internally <i>obsessed</i> with &quot;existential risk&quot;, to the point where they will cut off users. I do think this is genuine based on everything we&#x27;ve heard from Anthropic employees.<p>And of course, this safety culture likely persists in part because it&#x27;s <i>also</i> useful to the company&#x27;s overall strategy, in the same way Apple&#x27;s focus on user privacy, while likely genuine, conveniently lets them maintain control of the App Store.","created_at":"2026-11-01T16:36:13Z","created_at_i":1793550973,"objectID":"50327183","parent_id":50321207,"story_id":50319358,"story_title":"Anthropic blocks users for anything touching training larger models","story_url":"https://twitter.com/jiahaoshao1/status/2116475922190922088","updated_at":"2026-11-01T16:41:25Z"}],"hitsPerPage":3,"nbHits":28,"nbPages":10,"page":0,"params":"query=Claude+Code+pricing&tags=%28story%2Ccomment%29&hitsPerPage=3&page=0&numericFilters=created_at_i%3E%3D1791004054%2Ccreated_at_i%3C%3D1793596054&advancedSyntax=true&analyticsTags=backend","processingTimeMS":11,"processingTimingsMS":{"_request":{"roundTrip":18},"fetch":{"query":9,"total":10},"total":11},"query":"Claude Code pricing","serverTimeMS":12}
